 "title": "One thread, many users: Node.js",
 "sources": [
  "Node.js docs, 'Don't Block the Event Loop' (https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/dont-block-the-event-loop, fetched 2026-11-24): 'Node.js runs JavaScript code in the Event Loop... and offers a Worker Pool to handle expensive tasks like file I/O'; 'one Event Loop... and a pool of k Workers'; the Event Loop fulfils 'non-blocking asynchronous requests... e.g., network I/O'; 'uses a small number of threads to handle many clients'; 'While a thread is blocked working on behalf of one client, it cannot handle requests from any other clients'; 'Node.js is fast when the work associated with each client at any given time is small'; example: an n-squared loop inside a request handler."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Node.js runs your JavaScript on one thread. So how does it serve so many users? Guess.",
   "caps": "*ONE THREAD | SO MANY *USERS? | *GUESS"
  },
  {
   "text": "The trick: it never waits. When a request needs the network or a file, Node hands that off.",
   "caps": "NEVER *WAITS | *HANDS IT OFF"
  },
  {
   "text": "Network calls are non-blocking, and slow file work goes to a small worker pool.",
   "caps": "NETWORK: *NON-BLOCKING | FILES: *WORKER POOL"
  },
  {
   "text": "Meanwhile, the event loop moves straight on to the next request.",
   "caps": "*EVENT LOOP | NEXT *REQUEST"
  },
  {
   "text": "When the data is ready, its callback runs. Small pieces of work, many clients, few threads.",
   "caps": "DATA *READY | *CALLBACK RUNS | FEW *THREADS"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: one slow callback blocks everyone.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | ONE *SLOW CALLBACK | BLOCKS *EVERYONE"
  },
  {
   "text": "A big loop on the main thread, and no other user gets a turn until it ends.",
   "caps": "A *BIG LOOP | NO ONE GETS A *TURN"
  },
  {
   "text": "So keep each callback small, and move heavy work off the loop. Did you guess right?",
   "caps": "KEEP IT *SMALL | HEAVY WORK *OFF | DID YOU GUESS *RIGHT?"
  }
 ],
